{"id":"d3977639-177c-4454-ac2d-912eed21d0dd","object":"chat.completion","created":1785033235,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        node_map[n[\\\"id\\\"]] = n\\n        children.setdefault(n[\\\"parent\\\"], []).append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        if sym in required_symbols:\\n            counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            root_id = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n            break\\n    candidates = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"id\\\"] == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if not n[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if n[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        candidates.append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda id_: (-node_map[id_][\\\"depth\\\"], id_))\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for cid in candidates:\\n        if cid in removed_set:\\n            continue\\n        n = node_map[cid]\\n        child_ids = children.get(cid, [])\\n        has_retained_child = any(ch not in removed_set for ch in child_ids)\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        sym = n[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        if sym in required_symbols and counts.get(sym, 0) <= 1:\\n            continue\\n        removed_set.add(cid)\\n        removed_order.append(cid)\\n        if sym in required_symbols:\\n            counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_ids = sorted([n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes if n[\\\"id\\\"] not in removed_set])\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. From the description, it's \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" with a bounded objective: reduce a parse tree by removing nodes while preserving required symbols. The input case has a list of nodes with parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Required symbols: list of symbols that must remain in the tree. The algorithm: deeper nodes first, then by utility? But description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" Also \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" And \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\"\n\nWe need to decide which nodes to remove. The goal is to reduce the tree (remove nodes) but not all removable nodes; we need to preserve required symbols. Also we must ensure that after removal, the tree is still \"grammar valid\"? The description mentions \"grammar_valid\" as a hard contract. But we don't have grammar rules; we only have parent references. Possibly \"grammar valid\" means that after removal, the remaining nodes still form a valid tree (connected, parent-child relationships consistent). Since we only remove nodes and not restructure, if we remove a node, its children become disconnected? Typically in tree reduction, you can delete a node and its children are orphaned. But the description says \"Never remove a node with retained children\". So we cannot remove a node that still has children that are not removed. That implies we must remove leaf nodes first (or nodes whose entire subtree is removed). But it says \"Consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes\", so we process in depth order, and if a node has children, we can only remove it after all its children are removed (since they are deeper). But also \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a child is retained, we cannot remove the parent. So essentially we can remove a node only if all its descendants are also removed. So we need to decide a set of nodes to remove such that: the root is never removed; nodes with removable=true can be removed; but if a node is removed, its entire subtree must also be removed (since children would be orphaned, but if children are removed, that's fine). Actually if we remove a node, we must also remove its children? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if we remove a node, we are allowed to also remove its children, but we cannot remove a node while keeping some children. So the removal set must be a set of nodes such that no node in the set has an ancestor not in the set? Wait: If we remove a node, its children become disconnected. The specification says we can remove a node only if its children are also removed? Actually \"never remove a node with retained children\" means that if any child is retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove that node. So the removal set must be \"downward closed\": if a node is removed, all its descendants must also be removed. Equivalently, the retained nodes form a rooted subtree (connected to root). So we can only remove entire subtrees (prune branches). And we remove subtrees that are rooted at a removable node, provided the root is not removed and required symbols are still present.\n\nSo algorithm: For each node in decreasing depth (deepest first), check if it is removable and if its removal (i.e., removing the entire subtree rooted at this node) would cause any required symbol to be missing from the tree. If not, then we can remove it. But also we need to consider that after removing a subtree, the parent's children list becomes empty, maybe later the parent becomes removable. The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\", so we process from deepest to shallowest, and tentatively remove if conditions hold. This is like a greedy leaf removal. But we must also consider utility? The input has utility values. The description mentions \"utility\" but no explicit objective. The task says \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But we don't have an oracle; required symbols may be the oracle. Possibly we aim to maximize removed nodes (or minimize retained) while preserving required symbols, using depth-first removal. Or maybe we want to remove nodes that have higher utility? But utility is not mentioned in the contracts. The description says \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" So we must strictly follow order: deeper first, then utility? But \"registered_candidate_order\" might mean processing nodes in some order given by the input? The nodes are not necessarily in order. The description: \"registered_candidate_order\" as a hard contract – maybe it means we must process candidates in the order they appear? Or that we must consider nodes in the order of their ids? Not clear.\n\nLet's look at the fixture cases. They have 7 nodes: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6. Required symbols: root, s1. So any node that provides symbol s1 must be retained. Which nodes have symbol s1? n1 (parent root) and n4 (parent n2). Also root has symbol root. So nodes with required symbols: root, n1, n4. Those cannot be removed regardless of removable flag? \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". So n1 and n4 are not removable (n1 removable=false anyway, n4 removable=true but it's required symbol, so cannot be removed). So we cannot remove n4. n6 is removable, symbol s0, not required. n2 is removable, symbol s2, not required. n3 is not removable, symbol s0. n5 is not removable, symbol s2. So the only removable nodes are n2, n4, n6. But n4 is required so can't remove. So candidates: n2 and n6. Deeper first: n6 depth 2, n2 depth 1. Process n6 first. Can we remove n6? Its parent is n1 (required). Removing n6 would not affect required symbols (s1 still in n1 and n4). So we can remove n6. Then process n2: can we remove n2? It has child n4 which is required and retained. So removing n2 would leave n4 orphaned (parent removed but child retained). That violates \"never remove a node with retained children\". So we cannot remove n2 because its child n4 is retained. So only n6 is removed. Then retained nodes: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. But n2 is retained, n4 is retained, n5 is retained. That seems plausible. But is n3 removable? No, removable false. So removal set: [n6]. Retained set: all others. But the problem expects a specific output. We need to see the expected output? Not given. But we can infer from the task: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to output removed_node_ids (list of IDs removed, in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of IDs retained). For the first case, removable: n2, n4, n6 but n4 required. So only n6 removable. That yields removed_node_ids: [\"n6\"], retained_node_ids: all others sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"].\n\nNow second case: similar structure, utility values differ but same topology and required symbols. n1, n4 have symbol s1. n6 depth 2, n2 depth 1. n4 required, so only n6 removable. So same result.\n\nThird case: same.\n\nSo the output seems deterministic. But we need to implement the algorithm generically.\n\nSteps:\n1. Build a mapping from id to node dict. Also build children adjacency for each parent id.\n2. Determine which nodes are 'required' by symbol: if node's symbol is in required_symbols set, it cannot be removed.\n3. Determine depth order: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id? The description says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for nodes with same depth, we need a deterministic order. They didn't specify ascending or descending for ID. Usually tie break ascending. We'll assume ascending alphabetical/numeric order. Since IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc., ascending.\n4. For each node in that order:\n   - If node is root (parent null) or required symbol, skip.\n   - If node's removable flag is False, skip.\n   - If node has any child that is retained (i.e., not removed), skip (cannot remove node with retained children).\n   - Additionally, we must ensure that removing this node and its entire subtree (which is already all children removed because we process depth-first) does not cause any required symbol to be missing. But since we are removing leaf subtrees, and we have already ensured no required symbols in that subtree? Actually we need to check: before removal, does the subtree rooted at this node contain any required symbol? If yes, then removal would lose that required symbol, so we cannot remove. Because we cannot remove a node that has a required descendant. But we remove only when all children are already removed, so if a required node is deeper, it would not be removed (since required nodes are not removable), so they would still be present, and we cannot remove the parent. So we must check: node's subtree contains any retained node that is required? Since we are removing the entire subtree, if any required node exists in subtree (including node itself if required), we cannot remove. But node itself is not required (we skip if required). So check if any descendant (already retained) is required. Since we process deepest first, required nodes will be retained, so they prevent removal of ancestors.\n   - If passes, mark node as removed, add to removed list in order.\n5. After processing all, retained nodes are all nodes not removed. Sort their IDs alphabetically.\n6. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained_ids}.\n\nNote: The algorithm says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This means after hypothetical removal, check that the set of symbols from retained nodes still covers required_symbols. That is equivalent to ensuring the subtree does not contain any node whose symbol is required and which would be removed. Since required nodes are not removable, the only way a required symbol is lost is if the only node providing that symbol is in the removed subtree. So we need to track which nodes provide each required symbol. Since symbols can appear multiple times (e.g., s1 appears in n1 and n4), we can remove one but not both. So we must check if after removal, there is still at least one node for each required symbol. That is more complex: if there are multiple nodes with same required symbol, removing one is okay if another remains. So we need to count occurrences of each required symbol across retained nodes. Initially, we can compute counts. When we tentatively remove a node, we decrement count for its symbol if the node is not already removed. If after removal any required symbol count drops to zero, we cannot remove. So we need to simulate.\n\nBut note: we only process nodes deeper first, so when considering removal of a node, its descendants have already been considered (some may be removed already, some may be retained). So the counts already reflect removals that have been applied. We need to check if removing this node (which is a leaf in the current retained set) would cause any required symbol count to become zero. If yes, we cannot remove. This is like greedy removal: we try to remove as many as possible while preserving at least one instance of each required symbol.\n\nAlso, we must ensure that we don't remove a node that has retained children. That's already handled by processing depth-first: children deeper are processed first, so if children were not removed, they remain retained, and then parent cannot be removed because it has retained children. That matches condition.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Parse input: case dict has keys: \"nodes\", \"required_symbols\", maybe others.\n- Build dict by id.\n- Build children list for each node (or use parent to compute).\n- Determine ordered list of candidate nodes: all nodes that are removable (flag True), not root, and not required symbol. Sort by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n- Initialize counts: for each required symbol, count number of nodes in the full tree that have that symbol. But note: some nodes may be required in the sense of being the only instance? Actually required_symbols is a list of symbols that must be present in the tree after reduction. So we need to ensure at least one node with each required symbol remains. So we start with full counts, and as we remove nodes, we decrement counts.\n- Initialize set of removed IDs.\n- For each candidate in order:\n   - If node's id is already removed (should not be), skip.\n   - Check if node has any children that are not removed. Since we process deeper first, children are either already removed or not. If any child is not removed, skip (can't remove parent with retained children). This is equivalent to: for each child in children list, if child id not in removed, skip.\n   - Also: check symbol count: after removing this node, would any required symbol count become zero? For each required symbol, if the node's symbol == that symbol and the count of that symbol before removal is 1 (meaning this node is the last one), then we cannot remove. But careful: if node is not required symbol, it doesn't affect counts. So: if node's symbol in required_symbols and counts[node.symbol] == 1, skip removal.\n   - If passes, add node.id to removed set, decrement count for node.symbol if it's in required_symbols, append node.id to removed_order.\n- After loop, retained = [n for n in nodes if n.id not in removed_set]\n- Sort retained IDs alphabetically (string sort).\n- Return dict with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe need to handle that some nodes are not removable (removable=false) and are not candidates. Also root is not candidate.\n\nEdge cases: What about nodes that are required but not removable? They are not candidates, and we never remove them. So counts are decremented only for nodes we actually remove. That's fine.\n\nThe output structural schema requires exactly two arrays: removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted). Both arrays of strings.\n\nNow we need to write the Python function solve(case) that returns a dict.\n\nWe also must not mutate case. We'll copy data.\n\nCheck the fixture: There is \"_case_nonce\" field, but we ignore it.\n\nImplementation: Use only standard library.\n\nLet's code.\n\nWe need to be careful with the order of nodes in the input list. The problem says \"registered_candidate_order\" hard contract. The order we process is by depth descending then id. That matches \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So that is the order.\n\nWe also need to handle ties at depth: ID ascending. We'll sort using key = (-depth, id). Since depth is numeric.\n\nTesting with the given fixtures: We'll simulate mentally. For case 0: nodes:\nroot depth0, n1 depth1 (s1, removable false), n2 depth1 (s2, true), n3 depth1 (s0, false), n4 depth2 (s1, true), n5 depth2 (s2, false), n6 depth2 (s0, true). Required symbols: root, s1.\nCandidates: n6 depth2, n2 depth1, n4 depth2 (but n4 symbol s1 is required, so we should not consider it as candidate because it's required? Actually required symbols are those that must be preserved, so even if the node is removable, we should not remove it. The instruction says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". So we should not even attempt to remove nodes with required symbols. So we filter candidates: only removable and not required symbol. So n4 is omitted because its symbol is required. So candidates: n6, n2. n6 processed first: children? n6 has no children (depth2, may have none? In the tree, n6 is leaf. So no retained children. Counts: required symbols counts: root has 1 occurrence (root), s1 has 2 occurrences (n1 and n4). n6 symbol s0 not required, so no effect on counts. So remove n6. Then n2: children? n2 has child n4 (required, not removed). So n2 has retained child (n4), so cannot remove. So removed_order: [\"n6\"], retained: all others sorted.\nThat matches earlier.\n\nFor case 1, similar.\n\nNow we need to produce the final JSON with implementation.\n\nAlso note: The task mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". We are only doing deletion, not replacement. So that's fine.\n\nPotential nuance: The nodes dictionary includes \"utility\", but we don't use it. That's fine.\n\nNow write the code.\n\nWe'll include necessary imports: nothing needed.\n\nThe function:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        node_map[n[\"id\"]] = n\n        children.setdefault(n[\"parent\"], []).append(n[\"id\"])\n    # Ensure children for root\n    # Compute initial counts for required symbols\n    counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        if sym in required_symbols:\n            counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Build candidate list: removable, not root, not required symbol\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is None:\n            root_id = n[\"id\"]\n            break\n    candidates = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"id\"] == root_id:\n            continue\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if n[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        candidates.append(n[\"id\"])\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda id_: (-node_map[id_][\"depth\"], id_))\n    removed_set = set()\n    removed_order = []\n    for cid in candidates:\n        if cid in removed_set:\n            continue\n        n = node_map[cid]\n        # Check children not removed\n        child_ids = children.get(cid, [])\n        has_retained_child = any(ch not in removed_set for ch in child_ids)\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check if removal would eliminate a required symbol\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        if sym in required_symbols and counts.get(sym, 0) <= 1:\n            continue  # would lose last occurrence\n        # Remove\n        removed_set.add(cid)\n        removed_order.append(cid)\n        if sym in required_symbols:\n            counts[sym] -= 1\n    # Retained nodes\n    retained_ids = sorted([n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if n[\"id\"] not in removed_set])\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n\nCheck: Node with parent null is root. For children we used dict of list. For nodes with no children, children.get returns empty list.\n\nPotential issue: When we check counts, we decrement only if sym in required_symbols. But if node symbol is not required, we don't need to track. That's fine.\n\nNow we must ensure we don't mutate case. We are not mutating.\n\nNow put the code inside a JSON string for the \"implementation\" key.\n\nWe need to produce exactly one JSON object with \"implementation\" key. The value is a string of the source code.\n\nWe must escape newlines etc. We'll write the code as a triple-quoted string then use json.dumps to escape properly, but since we are to output the entire response as JSON, we can manually escape. Better to construct dict and then json.dumps it.\n\nBut the instruction says: \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So we need to produce a valid JSON string that contains the Python source. In the final output, the whole thing is a JSON object with key \"implementation\". We can use Python's json module to dump the dict, but we are writing the response text manually. Since we are an LLM, we can just output the JSON with proper escaping.\n\nWe'll write the code as a string with newlines replaced by \\n, backslashes double-escaped, quotes escaped. But it's easier to use json.dumps in our mind: we'll write the code as a normal Python string with triple quotes, then escape.\n\nAlternatively, we can output using a Python script? But we are to output the final JSON directly.\n\nWe'll construct the entire JSON string.\n\nLet's write the code as a function with proper indentation. Then we'll escape.\n\nWe'll use:\n\nimport json\n\ncode = '''\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        node_map[n[\"id\"]] = n\n        children.setdefault(n[\"parent\"], []).append(n[\"id\"])\n    counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        if sym in required_symbols:\n            counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is None:\n            root_id = n[\"id\"]\n            break\n    candidates = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"id\"] == root_id:\n            continue\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if n[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        candidates.append(n[\"id\"])\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda id_: (-node_map[id_][\"depth\"], id_))\n    removed_set = set()\n    removed_order = []\n    for cid in candidates:\n        if cid in removed_set:\n            continue\n        n = node_map[cid]\n        child_ids = children.get(cid, [])\n        has_retained_child = any(ch not in removed_set for ch in child_ids)\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        if sym in required_symbols and counts.get(sym, 0) <= 1:\n            continue\n        removed_set.add(cid)\n        removed_order.append(cid)\n        if sym in required_symbols:\n            counts[sym] -= 1\n    retained_ids = sorted([n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if n[\"id\"] not in removed_set])\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n'''\n\nThen output = json.dumps({\"implementation\": code})\n\nBut we need to output the JSON, not the dict. So we print the output of json.dumps. However, as an AI, I will manually produce the JSON string with proper escaping.\n\nI'll produce the final answer.\n\nNote: Ensure no trailing spaces.\n\nNow output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2057,"completion_tokens":5536,"total_tokens":7593,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5041},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":9},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}